<h2>What stays the same wherever we work</h2>
<p>The process does not change with the ZIP code: a Free Claim Review, a written contract with fees disclosed before any work begins, a thorough inspection, careful documentation, and steady communication with your insurer. Texas law caps public adjuster compensation at 10% of the insurance settlement, and you may rescind the contract within 72 hours of signing.</p>
<p>The region shares a few insurance realities too:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Not in the TWIA area.</strong> Wind and hail coverage typically comes from your regular insurer, often with a separate percentage wind/hail deductible.</li>
<li><strong>Flood is separate.</strong> Standard policies generally exclude flood, which matters in Flash Flood Alley. Flood coverage comes from the NFIP or a private flood policy.</li>
<li><strong>Permits vary by jurisdiction.</strong> San Marcos, Kyle, Buda, Wimberley, Dripping Springs, New Braunfels, Lockhart, and Seguin each run their own permit office, and Hays County does not issue residential building permits in unincorporated areas.</li>
</ul>
<p>As a Hays County public adjuster, we handle <a href="/services/hail-damage-claims/">hail</a>, <a href="/services/wind-damage-claims/">wind</a>, <a href="/services/roof-damage-insurance-claims/">roof</a>, <a href="/services/flood-damage-claims/">flood</a>, <a href="/services/freeze-damage-claims/">freeze</a>, <a href="/services/fire-damage-claims/">fire</a>, and <a href="/services/water-damage-claims/">water damage</a> claims for <a href="/services/residential-property-claims/">residential</a> and <a href="/services/commercial-property-claims/">commercial</a> properties. Not sure whether we cover your area? Request a <a href="/free-claim-review/">Free Claim Review</a> or call [[phone]].</p>
